    "**The table**\n",
    "- 300,000 loans (50,000 per year), one row per loan, 25 columns: loan id, year and group; the target; two modification flags (for analysis only); and 19 features (10 numbers, 7 categories and 2 \"was missing\" flags).\n",
    "- Train (2002-2004): 150,000 loans, 1,048 defaults (0.70%). Transition (2005): 50,000 loans, 526 defaults (1.05%). Test (2006-2007): 100,000 loans, 3,417 defaults (3.42%).\n",
    "- No loan was dropped for an unknown outcome.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What the checks show**\n",
    "- Loan ids are unique, the target is only 0 or 1, and 2005 is never in the training group.\n",
    "- For a sample of 2005 loans (random loans, plus defaults and modified loans) I worked out the target again with simple step-by-step logic. It matched the table for all 228 loans checked.\n",
    "- The \"Not Available\" codes are now blanks: credit score for 597 loans, debt-to-income for 6,596 (2.2%), and a few others (see the counts above). Channel `9` (9 loans) and property type `99` (32 loans) are blanks too.\n",
    "- The features carry signal. In the training years, loans that defaulted have a lower median credit score (649 against 736), a higher loan-to-value (80 against 72), a higher debt-to-income ratio (37 against 32) and a smaller balance.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Modified loans**\n",
    "- They stay in the table with two flags. The flags are **not** features, because a modification happens after the loan starts.\n",
    "- A loan modified inside the 24 months is almost always a default already (see the last table in section 5). If the flag were a feature, the model would be given the answer.\n",
    "- Modified loans are rare in the calm years and more common later: modified at any time is about 0.7% of the 2002 loans and 6.2% of the 2007 loans.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cautions**\n",
    "- The table is provisional. Dropping the seller name, the metropolitan area and the postal code was a judgement call, and the state (54 values) is not grouped yet.\n",
    "- The training years have only 1,048 defaults, which is not many for a flexible model.\n",
    "- Debt-to-income is blank for 2.2% of loans. Gradient boosting can use blanks as they are, but logistic regression needs them filled in.\n",
    "- The 24-month window of a 2006 or 2007 loan falls in the downturn, so \"loan year\" and \"economic conditions\" are tied together. That is the point of the study, but it also means I cannot tell a change in loan quality apart from a change in the economy."
   ]
