    "The table has 300,000 loans (50,000 a year) and 25 columns: loan id, year and group, the target, two modification flags (for analysis only) and 19 features (10 numbers, 7 categories and 2 \"was missing\" flags). Train (2002-2004) has 150,000 loans and 1,048 defaults (0.70%), the transition year 2005 has 50,000 and 526 (1.05%), and test (2006-2007) has 100,000 and 3,417 (3.42%). No loan was dropped for an unknown outcome.\n",
    "\n",
    "The checks pass. Loan ids are unique, the target is 0 or 1, and 2005 is never in the training group. For a sample of 2005 loans (random ones plus defaults and modified loans) I worked out the target again with simple step-by-step logic and it matched the table for all 228. The \"Not Available\" codes are now blanks (credit score 597 loans, debt-to-income 6,596, a few others), including channel `9` (9 loans) and property type `99` (32 loans). The features carry signal: in the training years, defaulted loans have a lower median credit score (649 against 736), a higher loan-to-value (80 against 72), a higher debt-to-income (37 against 32) and a smaller balance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Modified loans stay in the table with two flags, but the flags are not features, because a modification happens after the loan starts. A loan modified inside the 24 months is almost always a default already (the last table in section 5 shows it), so using the flag would hand the model the answer. Modified loans are rare in the calm years and more common later (about 0.7% of the 2002 loans, 6.2% of the 2007 loans).\n",
    "\n",
    "Cautions:\n",
    "- The table is provisional. Dropping the seller name, the metropolitan area and the postal code was a judgement call, and the state (54 values) isn't grouped here.\n",
    "- The training years have only 1,048 defaults, which isn't many for a flexible model.\n",
    "- Debt-to-income is blank for 2.2% of loans. Gradient boosting can use blanks as they are, but logistic regression needs them filled in.\n",
    "- The 24-month window of a 2006 or 2007 loan is inside the downturn, so loan year and economic conditions are tied together. That is the point of the study, but it means I can't separate a change in loan quality from a change in the economy."
